1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,390
Right, Jason and Karin, now I asked you to look at the case study for Box Telecom as part of your exam assessment.

2
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:12,241
It's interesting because they are in the middle of problems at the moment

3
00:00:12,241 --> 00:00:15,500
and I want you to track how they deal with them.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,835
Let's start with you, Karin. Having read through the case study,

5
00:00:19,835 --> 00:00:26,140
can you just summarise what the problems were that Box Telecom had to take on board?

6
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:31,880
Um, yeah... Well of course what first came to their attention was that

7
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:33,955
despite a new advertising campaign,

8
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:39,600
they were suffering from falling sales, and this is something that had many causes.

9
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,200
On top of that immediate problem

10
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:45,344
what had also happened over the last two years was that,

11
00:00:45,344 --> 00:00:48,140
although they had invested in an expansion plan

12
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,750
they had to face up to increased competition.

13
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,880
And, before they had a chance to get to grips with the effects of that

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:57,123
they were stalled by a strike

15
00:00:57,123 --> 00:01:02,889
and it was just when they were thinking about making a colossal investment in new machinery for their plants.

16
00:01:02,889 --> 00:01:04,710
So they were really in trouble.

17
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:10,121
Yes, I think that's fair. And Jason, you contacted the company, didn't you?

18
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:14,700
What did the company define as the reasons for these problems?

19
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,260
Well, I think they've hit on the right things

20
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:19,952
It would be easy to say they had invested too heavily,

21
00:01:19,952 --> 00:01:23,520
or at the wrong time, but in fact the signs were good

22
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,310
and what they were set back by was high interest rates.

23
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:29,460
At the same time, their longer-term problems

24
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:30,856
which were affecting their market share,

25
00:01:30,856 --> 00:01:33,700
were eventually credited to poor training

26
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:37,500
and having looked at the details in their last report I think that's right.

27
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:43,120
So, onto the larger issues then. Karin, what do you think the company will do?

28
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:54,950
Hmm... Well, obviously they have the choice of accepting the very favourable terms that another company-KMG Plc-have given them to buy them out.

29
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:58,930
That would mean creating a new company with a new image.

30
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:04,720
Or they could decide on a bolder move and offer some new shares if they wanted.

31
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:09,120
But I think they're much more cautious than that

32
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:10,689
and expect they will start trying to find individuals

33
00:02:10,689 --> 00:02:15,000
who'd be prepared to back them with some of the capital they need.

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,042
Well, you mustn't always assume that dramatic problems require dramatic solutions.

35
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:26,000
Sometimes there's a simple fix such as changing the guy at the top.

36
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,780
If they truly are cautious

37
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:32,930
then I suspect they will seek to shut down some of their shops.

38
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:35,380
But a more ambitious approach

39
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:39,591
and one which I think would have more chance of success,

40
00:02:39,591 --> 00:02:41,680
would be to alter how they're running things

41
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:45,004
The management layers and the processes.

42
00:02:45,004 --> 00:02:50,000
So in your analysis try to think of all the options. Jason?

43
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,984
Yes, it's interesting because I've found it a really useful company to study.

44
00:02:54,984 --> 00:02:59,969
Its problems cross all types of industries and it's lucky it's so big -

45
00:02:59,969 --> 00:03:04,570
a smaller or even medium-sized company would have gone under by now.

46
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:09,780
Ah well in fact, what I want you two to do is to go away

47
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,880
when we've finished our discussion today and write a report.

48
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:19,078
We've looked in general at the telecommunications market in the UK over the last few sessions

49
00:03:19,078 --> 00:03:23,339
and I want you to take Box Telecom as an example

50
00:03:23,339 --> 00:03:27,600
and suggest some ways in which they might overcome their problems.

51
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:32,185
And outline the reasons why you think as you do, but try

52
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:39,270
and keep it intrinsic to the company rather than dragging in other examples. Is that OK, Karin?

53
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:42,500
Yes, I think I can do that

54
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:46,570
Personally I've got great hopes for it. I think it will recover.

55
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:51,601
That advertising campaign they did was very strong and they're very innovative

56
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:54,480
with their products. They set new trends

57
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,890
The company's got to recover, don't you think, Jason?

58
00:03:57,890 --> 00:04:00,450
Hmmm. I'm not sure. I think it can

59
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:06,300
but it's not a foregone conclusion unless they manage to attract the right level of investment.

60
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,364
The company definitely needs a boost

61
00:04:08,364 --> 00:04:13,180
and to attract more highly skilled workers if their recovery is to be long-lasting.

62
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:16,060
When I was talking to the marketing manager

63
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:19,560
he said to me that he thinks the company had got a great management team

64
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,677
but he would say that, wouldn't he?

65
00:04:21,677 --> 00:04:24,021
-but they are suffering from having to work

66
00:04:24,021 --> 00:04:27,830
with outdated production machinery and that could cost a lot to put right.

67
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:31,400
Well, personally I think the stock market is to blame

68
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,443
I think they were expecting too much of the company

69
00:04:34,443 --> 00:04:37,750
and then inevitably it looked bad when it didn't perform.

70
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:40,855
The market should have had more realistic expectations.

71
00:04:40,855 --> 00:04:44,736
And I disagree with you about the advertising campaign Karin.

72
00:04:44,736 --> 00:04:49,005
That's where they could do with some innovation-to get sales kick-started.

73
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:52,110
Anyway, let's see what you come up with...
